PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The clinical vignette describes sequential antiepileptic therapy culminating in Stevens-Johnson syndrome or toxic epidermal necrolysis, manifested by cutaneous blisters. The primary initiating pharmacokinetic reaction is the inhibition of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase-mediated glucuronidation, commonly seen when valproate blocks lamotrigine clearance. Concurrently, the specific enzymatic bioactivation reaction producing the reactive intermediate responsible for protein haptenation and immune-mediated keratinocyte apoptosis is cytochrome P450-mediated epoxidation into reactive arene oxides. Both glucuronidation (inhibition) and epoxidation remain the central candidate answers to be preserved across deliberation rounds.
}

